{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And he prayed</b> — The commentators sought to interpret an innovation and removed the verse from its plain sense, for Jonah did not pray until after he emerged onto dry land, because it is found written \"from the belly\" (ממעי) and not \"in the belly\" (במעי). But did they not see there written \"from the womb I cried out\" (מבטן שועתי), and likewise \"from the depths I called you, YHWH\" (ממעמקים קראתיך ה')? Moreover: the word \"I cried out\" (שועתי) is a sign that he prayed and cried out to HaShem before the fish vomited him, and likewise: \"and your prayer came to you\" (ותבא אליך תפילתי). And behold, why would it not have been written \"and Jonah prayed after he emerged from the belly of the fish\"?! Moreover: after his prayer it is written: \"and YHWH said to the fish, and it brought them into this distress\" — on account of \"you brought up my life from the pit\" (ותעל משחת) and \"your prayer came to you\" (ותבא אליך תפלתי). And now set your mind and see: every prayer of a prophet and his blessing is in the spirit of prophecy. And Jacob said: \"I have taken from the hand of the Amorite,\" for a thing decreed to be, one speaks in the past tense. And likewise: \"he bowed his shoulder to bear,\" \"a star from Jacob,\" \"Jeshurun grew fat and kicked,\" \"YHWH saw and spurned,\" \"Israel dwelt safely, alone.\" And in David's prayer when he fled: \"He answered me from His holy sanctuary, selah\" — for Scripture says \"when he fled\" (בברחו) and not \"when he fled\" (ברחו) [n: the two forms differ only in matres lectionis; Ibn Ezra's point turns on the spelling distinction], and in his prayer: \"yet I will continue to gaze upon Your holy temple\" — which is the heavens, and \"YHWH is in His holy temple,\" and many like this. And there it is written: \"salvation belongs to YHWH\" — which was his hope, like \"salvation belongs to YHWH, to seek.\" And some say: a fish (דגה) swallowed the fish (דג), and there is no need, for דגה and דג are the species name, like צדק and צדקה."], :book :jonah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "The text reads בברחו twice with apparently only a minor orthographic difference between them; translated hyper-literally as the distinction is preserved but barely visible in transliteration."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Jonah -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/daat/olam_hatanah/mefaresh.asp?book=19&perek=1&mefaresh=ezra"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:07:18.860942571Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}